# Ironclad

Ironclad is a set of engineering standards, bundled alongside this file as
individual Markdown files in this same skill folder, including
`task-routing.md`, the table this skill uses to decide what to read. This
skill file routes to them; it does not duplicate their content.

## How to decide what to read

Consult `task-routing.md`, bundled alongside this file, to map the task at
hand to the file(s) that apply. Always read `ai-usage-policy.md` and
`language.md` regardless of task type, they are cross-cutting.

## Selecting the intensity level

Ironclad runs at one of three intensity levels, `core`, `full`, or
`max`, described in `intensity.md`. The level controls how much of the
adjustable, craft-depth guidance you load; it never drops the security
floor (`ai-usage-policy.md`, `language.md`, `security.md`, and `lgpd.md`
when personal data is involved), which loads at every level.

Determine the active level, highest precedence first:

1. An explicit `/ironclad core`, `/ironclad full`, or `/ironclad max`
   typed this session.
2. An `.ironclad` file at the project root whose first non-empty,
   non-comment line names the level.
3. The default, `full`, if neither is present.

Then apply it when reading `task-routing.md`: at `core`, read only the
single most task-specific file from the matching row; at `full`, read
every file the row points to; at `max`, additionally read
`review-checklist.md` and treat the coverage floor in `testing.md` as
mandatory. An unrecognized level falls back to `full`.

## When the user types `/ironclad` with no argument

Show the short index below and stop. This is a deliberate request to see
what is available, not a request to apply a rule yet.

| File | Covers |
|---|---|
| `language.md` | English-only rule for all project content and output. |
| `intensity.md` | The three intensity levels (core, full, max) and how the active level is selected. |
| `architecture.md` | Layered architecture, dependency rule, SOLID. |
| `backend.md` | Server-side validation, IDOR, mass assignment, error handling, sessions, passwords, CSRF, CORS, SSRF, path traversal, dynamic code execution. |
| `frontend.md` | XSS/output escaping, client-side storage, third-party script integrity, component structure. |
| `database.md` | Schema design, indexing, N+1 prevention, migrations, Row Level Security. |
| `security.md` | Pre-launch and ongoing security checklist. |
| `testing.md` | TDD, test scope, fragile tests, the coverage floor. |
| `debug.md` | Systematic debugging, local and production incidents. |
| `refactor.md` | Behavior-preserving change, characterization tests for legacy code. |
| `commits.md` | Conventional Commits format, message content and length. |
| `observability.md` | Structured logging, correlation IDs, log levels, what never to log. |
| `resilience.md` | Timeouts, retries, idempotency, graceful degradation for outbound calls. |
| `lgpd.md` | Personal data protection (LGPD, GDPR, or equivalent), with extra guidance when minors are among the project's users. |
| `ai-usage-policy.md` | Team accountability for AI-generated code, and how this assistant should operate. |
| `review-checklist.md` | The full code review checklist, what `/ironclad review` applies. |
| `quality-gate.md` | Automated pre-merge gate (compile/type and lint) that blocks undefined symbols and type errors, per stack. |
| `task-routing.md` | Table mapping task type to which files to read. |

## When you are invoking this skill yourself, automatically

Do not stop at the index in this case, that behavior is reserved for a
user typing the bare command. First determine the active intensity using
the rules above. Then match the task against `task-routing.md` and apply
that intensity to every matching row before reading its files: at
`core`, read only the single most task-specific file from each row; at
`full`, read every file the row points to; at `max`, also read
`review-checklist.md` and treat the coverage floor in `testing.md` as
mandatory. If the task spans more than one topic, for example an endpoint
that also queries the database, apply the active intensity to every
matching row, not just the first. Always read the security floor as
described above. If you are genuinely unsure which row applies, read
`review-checklist.md` as a safe general default rather than skipping
standards entirely.

## When an explicit topic argument is given

One or more of: `architecture`, `backend`, `frontend`, `database`,
`security`, `testing`, `observability`, `resilience`, `lgpd`, `debug`,
`refactor`, `commits`, `intensity`, `quality-gate`, `review` (maps to `review-checklist.md`). Read
every file named, plus `ai-usage-policy.md` and `language.md`, and
apply them to the current task. Multiple topics may be given together,
for example `/ironclad backend database`.

## `/ironclad review`

Read `review-checklist.md` and apply it to the current diff or recently
changed files.
